local storage and session storage
=both localstorage and sessonStorage are part of the web storage Api used to store key-value pairs in the browser
=store data in the browser (no server needed)
=improve user experience (keep user settings and user specific data)
=fast and easy access
=easy to use api
=foundation for advanced topics(indexDB, cookies, PWA)


well both are part of the web storage API and they allow you to store data in browser in key-value pair
its capacity is roughly between 5 to 10mb
the data is always stored in string
the retrieval is synchronous and faster than fetching from a server



local storage
=the data persists even if the browser/tab is closed
=shared across all tabs/windows of the same origin
=used in settings like dark mode or user perferences 

session storage
=data is cleared when the tab or window is closed
=limited to the specific tab where it was created
=used in temporary data like a single session form or ID




Essential Methods
The methods are identical for both localStorage and sessionStorage.
setItem(key, value): Saves data.
getItem(key): Retrieves data using the key.
removeItem(key): Deletes a specific item.
clear(): Deletes all data in that storage.
key(index): Gets the name of the key at a specific numeric index.
length: Property that returns the number of items stored.




Output Visualization
In the browser, you can see this data by:
Right-clicking and choosing Inspect.
Going to the Application tab.
Selecting Local Storage or Session Storage in the left sidebar.







Key Considerations (Production Tips)
Security: Never store sensitive data like passwords or private tokens here. They are easily accessible via JavaScript (vulnerable to XSS attacks).

Same-Origin Policy: Data is isolated by domain. For example, data saved on site-a.com cannot be accessed by site-b.com.

Profiles: Storage is browser and profile-specific. Data in Chrome's "Profile A" won't appear in Chrome's "Profile B" or in Incognito mode.

